				 In-Person Voting on Election Day 







	 Polling hours are from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM. 
	 Residents can search their precinct click:  www.sec.state.ma.us/wheredoivotema/ 
	 When you approach the check-in table, you have two options for checking in

		 Provide your first and last name 
		 Place your id on the scanner  (this is not required)  
		 Confirm your name and address by repeating them to the election worker.  Both you and the election worker should loudly say your name and address.  This allows any observers to hear the name and address. 


	 In a primary election, voters who are registered in a political party may only obtain a ballot for that party. All other voters may request the ballot of any party participating in the primary election. Your party affiliation will not change, based on the ballot you choose. 
	 An election worker will be at the ballot box/tabulator to ensure your ballot is accepted.  Election workers are not allowed to cast your ballot.  You need to wait until you hear the ballot drop in the ballot box 
	 Election officials are available to answer any questions about this part of the voting process.  
	 What to do if your name is not included on the voter rolls:

		 If your name does not appear on the list of voters, the Warden should contact the Election Commission Office to determine if you are registered in another precinct or different community. 
		 If you are considered an inactive voter, you will need to show ID and sign an affirmation to confirm your continued residency. 
		 If you believe you should be allowed to vote, but aren’t on the voter rolls, you can request a provisional ballot. Provisional Ballots allow you to cast your vote.  Provisional Ballots will be researched after the election.  If research shows you are eligible to vote, your ballot will be hand- tallied and included in the election results. 




















				 Vote by Mail 






			 ​​​​Massachusetts voters now have the option to vote early by mail in all Municipal, State and Federal Elections, with no justification required.  

 State and Federal Elections:   If you are already enrolled in vote by mail, you will receive a card that looks  like this , indicating your selected party. If you do not receive an application, but would like to vote by mail, you may submit the following  signed application  to the Election Commission Office.  

 Municipal Elections:  The state does  NOT  mail out application cards for Municipal Elections. If you would like to vote by mail in Municipal Elections, you must submit the following  signed application  to the Election Commission Office via mail, drop box, email, or in person. You can also apply online on the  www.sec.state.ma.us/ele/eleidx.htm . 

 All ballots must be returned to the Election Commission Office by the close of the polls (8:00PM), on election day. Ballots delivered to the polling location  cannot be accepted. 


	 RECEIVING AND RETURNING MY BALLOT 


		 Completed applications can be deposited in the secure ballot drop box located outside City Hall, mailed to the Election Commission Office at 85 George P. Hassett Dr, Room 102, or via email at  elections@medford-ma.gov . 
		 After receiving completed applications, the Election Commission Office will mail voters Early Vote by Mail ballot with a pre-paid postage return envelope. Watch the mail for your ballot package from the Election Commission Office. 
		 Return the completed ballot to the Election Commission Office, by 8:00pm the day of the election. Election Commission Office hours are Monday, Tuesday, Thursday 8:30 AM-4:30 PM, Wednesday 8:30 AM-7:30 PM, and Friday 8:30 AM-12:30 PM.  
		 Municipal election vote by mail ballots must be received by 8:00PM on election day.  
		 State and Federal election vote by mail ballots must be   received by 8:00PM on election day. 
		 Ballots delivered to the polling location  cannot be accepted. 
		 Questions can be directed to the Election Commission Office by email or by phone at  (781)-393-2490 . 




















				 Military & Overseas Citizens Voters/ Specially Qualified Voters 







	 The Uniformed and Overseas Citizens Absentee Voting Act (UOCAVA) protects the voting rights for Uniformed Services members and members of the US merchant marine, their families and all US citizens residing outside of the United States, and who is at least 18 years old. In Massachusetts, these voters are called Specially Qualified Voters. 
	 The primary form for requesting an absentee ballot for Specially Qualified Voters is the Federal Post Card Application (FPCA). 
	 Download and print the Federal Post Card Application-   www.fvap.gov/citizen-voter/overview 
	 Are eligible to vote in Medford, if it is the community where the citizen last resided immediately prior to departing the US. 
	 The Federal Voter Assistance Program : ( www.fvap.gov/citizen-voter/overview ) has complete instructions on filling out the application 
	 Remember  to include an email address. E-mail is the fastest way to communicate. 
	 The FPCA must be received in the Clerk’s Office by NOON the Monday before an election. 
	 FPCA forms may be mailed, faxed, or scanned and e-mailed to our office. 
	 Federal Write-In Absentee Ballot (FWAB) /www.fvap.gov/fwab-privacy-notice is only available to UOCAVA voters who have timely requested but not received their regular absentee ballot. 


















				 Permanent Absentee 






			 A voter who is a permanent absentee need not submit a request for an absentee ballot at every election. If such a voter submits a note from a registered physician indicating that he is disabled permanently, the Election Commission Office will send an application for an absentee ballot twenty-eight days before the first election each year. Upon the City Clerk's receipt of the signed application, the voter will be sent an absentee ballot for all elections that year.  Upon the receipt of the signed application, the voter will be sent an absentee ballot for all elections that year. 

 Download and print the physician letter form (.pdf) 

















				 Political Affiliation 






			 When you register to vote you may choose to register in a state political party, select a political designation (i.e., become affiliated with a political organization which has not received party status in Massachusetts) or unenrolled. Unenrolled is commonly referred to as "independent" and means you are not enrolled in a party but are a registered voter. You can vote in a party primary if you are listed as unenrolled.  You will be asked by an election worker which party's ballot you would like to receive. Your voter registration does not change based on the primary ballot chosen.  Unenrolled does not mean that you are not registered to vote. 

















				 Pre-Registration 







	 As of August 1, 2016, sixteen- and seventeen-year-olds may pre-register to vote. For further information on pre-registration see the  Elections Division (state.ma.us) 
	 Voters can check their  voter registration status  on the Secretary of State’s web site at  www.sec.state.ma.us  or by calling the Election Commission Office at  (781)-393-2490 .  
	 Be aware that there are voter registration deadlines. You must register to vote before any Local or State election or primary. For additional information call the Election Commission Office at  (781)-393-2490 .  


















				 Seeking Local Public Office 







	 Prior to obtaining nomination papers, each candidate must file a statement containing his/her name and address and the office for which he/she intends to run with the Election Commission Office. 
	 Before any voter may be permitted to sign a nomination paper, the candidate’s information must be filled out, including their name and address (number and street) as they appear on the current voting list and the office to which they seek nomination.  
	 To seek public office, you must be: 18 years of age, an American citizen, a registered voter, a resident of Medford. 
	 Certified signatures of at least fifty (50) voters are the minimum necessary for placement on the preliminary ballot for Mayor, City Council, and School Committee. The Election Commission need not certify more names than required plus 20%. 


 Preliminary Election - Determining Candidates for Municipal Election 

 "…there shall be held a preliminary election for the purpose of nominating candidates, if necessary, to assure that no more than twice the number of candidates for the available offices appear on the regular municipal election ballot." 

 After the deadline for turning in nomination papers has passed, it will be determined whether the City will hold a preliminary election based on the number of eligible candidates. All dates referring to the Preliminary Election are posted for planning purposes only and will be removed from the election calendar if it is determined that the Preliminary Election is not necessary. 

















				 Who May Register? 






			 Any person who is a Massachusetts resident, a United States citizen and will be eighteen years old as of the next election, is eligible to register to vote. First time voters in Massachusetts must provide a copy of their identification if registering to vote by mail. If no ID is provided, they will be required to show proof of residency at the polling location on election day. When signing the registration form, you must attest, under the pains and penalties of perjury, that you are legally eligible to register and that the information you provide is accurate and truthful. The penalty for fraudulent registration increased recently to a fine of ten thousand dollars or imprisonment for up to five years, or both. You will remain on the list of voters so long as you vote regularly and complete and return the annual municipal census. If you move to another city or City, you must re-register in your new community. 

















				 Have You Moved? 






			 You must update your voter registration every time you move, even within the same city. If you have moved, you can update your registration by filling out a new  voter registration form (PDF)  and returning it to the Election Commission Office or by making the change  online . 




























